            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nW. E. B. Du Bois was born on February 23, 1868, in Great Barrington, Massachusetts. He was the first African American to earn a Ph.D. from Harvard University in 1895. His early academic work laid the groundwork for his future role as a leading civil rights activist and scholar.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Souls of Black Folk</strong><br>\nIn 1903, Du Bois published \"The Souls of Black Folk,\" a seminal work in the history of sociology and African American literature. This collection of essays challenged the dominant ideologies of race and pushed for the political and social rights of African Americans.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding the NAACP</strong><br>\nDu Bois was one of the founders of the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) in 1909. He served as the organization's director of publicity and research and as the editor of its magazine, \"The Crisis,\" which became a platform for his views on civil rights.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Disagreement with Booker T. Washington</strong><br>\nDu Bois openly criticized Booker T. Washington's approach to racial issues, advocating instead for immediate civil rights, the pursuit of higher education, and political representation for African Americans.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pan-Africanism</strong><br>\nDu Bois was a leading proponent of Pan-Africanism and organized several Pan-African Congresses to address the issues facing Africa as a result of European colonization. He believed in the unity and solidarity of African people worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Academic Contributions</strong><br>\nAs a sociologist, Du Bois conducted extensive research on African American society. His work, including the Atlanta University Studies, was pivotal in challenging the myths and stereotypes about black communities.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
