          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life Activism</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Du Bois continued to advocate for civil rights and became increasingly critical of American capitalism and imperialism. His leftist views during the Cold War era made him a controversial figure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Emigration to Ghana</strong><br>\nDisillusioned with the progress of civil rights in America, Du Bois moved to Ghana at the invitation of President Kwame Nkrumah in 1961, to help with the creation of a new encyclopedia of the African diaspora, the Encyclopedia Africana.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Enduring Influence</strong><br>\nW. E. B. Du Bois died on August 27, 1963, in Accra, Ghana. His death came one day before Martin Luther King Jr.'s \"I Have a Dream\" speech. Du Bois's work remains influential in the discourse on race, economics, politics, and culture.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
